<section class="page-head">
<div>
<div class="eyebrow">Path One · Modernism Listening</div>
<h1>Listening to Blues &amp; Jazz</h1>
<p class="lede">Hear a pulse made alive by phrasing, voices that respond to one another, and a recurring form that lets each new statement become legible.</p>
</div>
<span class="page-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 56 56"><circle cx="28" cy="28" r="22" fill="none" stroke="var(--mark-primary)" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="28" cy="28" r="16" fill="none" stroke="var(--mark-primary)" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="2 2" opacity=".6"/><path d="M14 28 Q 21 18, 28 28" fill="none" stroke="var(--mark-primary)" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M28 28 Q 35 38, 42 28" fill="none" stroke="var(--mark-primary)" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="14" cy="28" r="1.1" fill="var(--mark-primary)"/><circle cx="42" cy="28" r="1.1" fill="var(--mark-primary)"/></svg></span>
</section>

<p class="threshold">Blues and jazz are distinct, historically connected African American traditions. These studies move between them to explore several shared practices without treating either tradition as one sound or one fixed set of rules.</p>
<section class="intro-ways"><h2>How to listen here</h2><p>A composer or performer shapes more than notes. Musicians organize time together, respond to one another, and make new statements inside shared forms. Each controlled study isolates one relationship; the historical connection lets you hear it operating inside a richer performance.</p></section>

