<p class="lens-idea"><strong>The musical idea:</strong> A chorus is one complete trip through the form organizing a jazz performance. In this study, that shared route is a twelve-bar blues framework made of three four-bar spans.</p>
<p class="lens-builds"><strong>How musicians use it:</strong> When the form begins again, a musician may restate the melody, vary it, improvise, leave space, or take a different rhythmic and melodic path. The common framework makes those choices easier to hear.</p>
<p class="lens-q"><em>What returns, and what does each voice do differently inside that return?</em></p>
<p class="lens-cue">An alto saxophone and a trumpet take different phrase paths through the same three-span foundation.</p>
